Wiring is an accepted design for parameter contracts. The current Alpha
compiler does not support Wire or Route syntax yet; that implementation is
planned for Phase 6.

The word before `of` chooses the meaning of the following list:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box of String
    value Wire of String
    submit Route of AppMessage
]

`Box of String` is an ordinary generic type application. `Wire of String` and
`Route of AppMessage` describe contracts on function parameters. They are not
runtime wrapper types, general values or constructor expressions.

Several Moth declarations use pipe-delimited lists, but their positions keep
different contracts:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    show |value String| -> String:
        return value
    ;

    Point = |
        x Int,
        y Int,
    |

    Status ::
        Ready,
        Failed | message String |,
    ;
]

Function parameters receive call arguments. Struct fields describe named
runtime value storage. Choice payloads describe immutable data carried by one
variant. Similar list shapes do not make these positions interchangeable:
Wire and Route contracts are parameters only, never fields or payloads.

Use the @../functions (Functions), @../structs (Structs),
@../choices (Choices) and @../generics (Generics) references for those existing
contracts. The detailed Wiring reference owns the accepted future syntax and
edge cases.

The receiving type must be immediate. The same boundary rule applies to
ordinary generic calls, optional values and casts:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    empty type Item || -> {Item}:
        return {}
    ;

    items {Int} = empty()
    maybe_name String? = none
    wide I64 = cast 1
]

`empty()` needs a concrete collection type, `none` needs an optional type, and
`cast` gets its target from the annotated receiving type. A later use cannot
fill in missing evidence, and an outer function parameter does not infer a
nested generic call. Annotate an intermediate value when needed. Wiring adds
no general higher-order inference or retry rule. See
@../generics/#generic-inference (Generic inference),
@../errors/#options (Options) and @../casts/#cast-syntax (Casts).
